# An interleaved log: both members are announced twice, once to build
# and once to test, so each member's output arrives in two pieces with
# the other member's in between.
#
# This is not what today's runner writes — it announces a member once
# and runs lint, test and install under that one marker — but it is the
# shape verify.SplitSubjects deliberately permits ("a runner that
# returns to a port ... loses neither half"), and a judge that assumed
# one marker per member would read the second half of jq's output as
# oniguruma's. The verdicts below are only reachable if the sections
# accumulate: jq's lint line is in its first piece and its failure is
# in its second, and both must land on jq.
#
# It also pins how far the runner is taken to have got. The stopper is
# the last member the FILE announces, so it is jq here — the guest was
# writing jq's section when it gave up, which is what a marker means.
# Reading the roster's order instead would agree in this file only
# because jq happens to sort last; interleaved-return-failure is the
# same shape with the failure on the return visit, where the two orders
# disagree and only one of them names the port that broke.
# SYNTHESIZED from verify.SplitSubjects's repeat-marker contract, not
# captured: see README.md.
members: oniguruma jq
outcome: failed
oniguruma.state: passed
oniguruma.lint: clean
jq.state: failed
jq.detail: Failed to test jq: command execution failed
